  heading="About PB&B"
  standfirst="A small-run label built around hand-drawn graphics, printed big enough to be the whole garment."
>
  <p>
    Every graphic in this store was drawn by hand — the nova star, the moth, the ironwork, the
    filigree that climbs the leg of the denim. Nothing is licensed clip art and nothing is
    generated. The work starts as a drawing and the garment is chosen to carry it.
  </p>

  <h2>How the clothes are made</h2>
  <p>
    Runs are small and sizes are cut from one block per garment type, so a Nova Star Tee and a Moth
    Tee fit identically. That is deliberate: once you know your size in one PB&amp;B tee, you know it
    in all of them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Print placement is specified rather than eyeballed. On the tees the chest wordmark runs 7 inches
    wide by 3 inches tall, and the back graphic runs 17 inches wide by 12 inches tall on a size L —
    those are the numbers on the production spec, not estimates.
  </p>

  <h2>Sizing, honestly</h2>
  <p>
    Fit is the single biggest reason clothes get sent back, so every garment is listed with flat
    measurements in inches. Lay something you already like next to the numbers rather than guessing
    from a letter. The size L tee is measured off the production sample; the rest of the run is
    graded from it, and the <a href="/size-guide">size guide</a> says which is which.
  </p>

